Write a query listing customers who have never placed an order, using a NULL-safe anti-join.

CREATE TABLE customers (cust_id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY, cust_name VARCHAR2(60) NOT NULL);
INSERT ALL
  INTO customers (cust_id, cust_name) VALUES (1, 'Acme Corp')
  INTO customers (cust_id, cust_name) VALUES (2, 'Globex')
  INTO customers (cust_id, cust_name) VALUES (3, 'Initech')
SELECT * FROM dual;
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY, cust_id NUMBER, order_dt DATE);
INSERT ALL
  INTO orders (order_id, cust_id, order_dt) VALUES (1001, 1, DATE '2026-01-05')
  INTO orders (order_id, cust_id, order_dt) VALUES (1002, 1, DATE '2026-02-01')
SELECT * FROM dual;

SELECT c.cust_id, c.cust_name
FROM customers c
WHERE NOT EXISTS (
  SELECT NULL FROM orders o WHERE o.cust_id = c.cust_id
);
NOT EXISTS is NULL-safe by construction: a customer simply has no matching order row, so NULL cust_id values in orders cause no special cases. The optimizer typically plans this as a HASH JOIN ANTI. Contrast with NOT IN, which returns zero rows the moment orders contains a NULL cust_id (the comparison cust_id = NULL evaluates to UNKNOWN for every row), and LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL, which needs the extra join-filter discipline to stay correct.

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE apply_interest(p_rate NUMBER) IS
  TYPE t_id IS TABLE OF accounts.acct_id%TYPE;
  l_ids t_id;
BEGIN
  SELECT acct_id
  BULK COLLECT INTO l_ids
  FROM accounts;

  FORALL i IN 1 .. l_ids.COUNT
    UPDATE accounts
       SET balance = balance * (1 + p_rate / 100)
     WHERE acct_id = l_ids(i);

  COMMIT;
END apply_interest;
/
BULK COLLECT pulls all keys into a collection in one fetch, and FORALL issues the updates in engine-batched mode rather than one context switch per row. This collapses N single-row UPDATE statements into a single bulk operation, typically a large multiple faster on big tables. For very large tables, add a LIMIT clause to the BULK COLLECT and loop in chunks so the collection never exceeds PGA budget. Since the procedure commits, callers must not wrap it in a transaction expecting to roll back its effects.

