Write a PL/SQL procedure that validates and logs a failed insert, using an autonomous transaction so the log survives a rollback.

CREATE TABLE audit_errors (error_id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY, error_msg VARCHAR2(4000), occurred_at DATE);
CREATE SEQUENCE seq_audit_errors START WITH 1 INCREMENT BY 1;

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE log_audit_error(p_msg VARCHAR2) IS
  PRAGMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION;
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO audit_errors (error_id, error_msg, occurred_at)
  VALUES (seq_audit_errors.NEXTVAL, p_msg, SYSDATE);
  COMMIT;
END log_audit_error;
/

The PRAGMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION makes this procedure run in its own independent transaction: it commits the log insert regardless of the calling transaction's fate. If the caller later rolls back, the audit row remains, which is exactly what error logging requires. The COMMIT inside is mandatory — an autonomous transaction must commit or roll back before returning control, or the log is lost. The cost of autonomy is real: the log writes outside the caller's transaction, so a batch that rolls back still shows logged errors for work that never committed, which is usually the desired audit semantics but must be understood when reconciling counts.

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE rename_if_exists(
  p_table VARCHAR2, p_old_col VARCHAR2, p_new_col VARCHAR2
) IS
  l_cnt PLS_INTEGER;
BEGIN
  SELECT COUNT(*)
    INTO l_cnt
    FROM user_tab_columns
   WHERE table_name = UPPER(p_table)
     AND column_name = UPPER(p_old_col);

  IF l_cnt > 0 THEN
    EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'ALTER TABLE ' || p_table ||
                      ' RENAME COLUMN ' || p_old_col || ' TO ' || p_new_col;
  ELSE
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Column ' || p_old_col || ' not found; no-op');
  END IF;
END rename_if_exists;
/

The procedure consults the data dictionary for the column before issuing the rename, so it never raises ORA-00904 on an already-renamed column — making repeated deployment of the same script idempotent. The dynamic DDL is required because ALTER TABLE cannot accept identifiers as bind variables. Identifier interpolation carries injection risk, so this must only be called with trusted literal arguments or validated with DBMS_ASSERT. The COUNT-based existence check is race-tolerant for a deployment script, though concurrent DDL on the same object could still collide.

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION fn_count_by(
  p_table VARCHAR2, p_col VARCHAR2, p_value VARCHAR2
) RETURN NUMBER
IS
  l_cnt NUMBER;
BEGIN
  EXECUTE IMMEDIATE
    'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM ' || DBMS_ASSERT.simple_sql_name(p_table)
    || ' WHERE ' || DBMS_ASSERT.simple_sql_name(p_col) || ' = :v'
    INTO l_cnt USING p_value;
  RETURN l_cnt;
END fn_count_by;
/

SELECT fn_count_by('REGION_ACCOUNTS', 'REGION_CD', 'EAST') AS east_count FROM dual;
DBMS_ASSERT.simple_sql_name validates that the interpolated identifiers are legal, non-quoted object names, which closes the SQL-injection hole that a raw concatenation would open. The column and table names are identifiers (validated), while the filter VALUE is passed as a bind variable through USING, keeping it out of the parsed statement entirely. This is the correct pattern for dynamic-SQL utilities that must accept identifier parameters without becoming injection vectors. The call returns 2 for the EAST region in the sample data.

Write a PL/SQL procedure that upserts a customer, inserting when absent and updating the name when present, returning the resulting ID.

CREATE TABLE customers_upsert (cust_id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY, cust_name VARCHAR2(60) NOT NULL, region_cd VARCHAR2(10));
INSERT INTO customers_upsert (cust_id, cust_name, region_cd) VALUES (1, 'Acme', 'EAST');

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE upsert_customer(
  p_cust_id NUMBER, p_cust_name VARCHAR2, p_region_cd VARCHAR2,
  p_result_id OUT NUMBER
) IS
BEGIN
  MERGE INTO customers_upsert t
  USING (SELECT p_cust_id AS cust_id FROM dual) s
  ON (t.cust_id = s.cust_id)
  WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET
       t.cust_name = p_cust_name, t.region_cd = p_region_cd
  WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (cust_id, cust_name, region_cd)
       VALUES (p_cust_id, p_cust_name, p_region_cd);
  p_result_id := p_cust_id;
  COMMIT;
END upsert_customer;
/

DECLARE
  l_id NUMBER;
BEGIN
  upsert_customer(2, 'Globex', 'WEST', l_id);
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Result id = ' || l_id);
END;
/
MERGE drives the upsert in a single statement: the MATCHED branch updates an existing row, the NOT MATCHED branch inserts a new one. The source is a single-row SELECT from DUAL carrying the parameters, a common trick for parameterized MERGE. The OUT parameter hands the caller the ID they just upserted, which equals the input primary key here. COMMIT inside the procedure means callers cannot roll back its effect as part of a larger unit; if that coupling matters, remove the COMMIT and let the caller manage the transaction.

DECLARE
  l_total PLS_INTEGER;
  l_shipped PLS_INTEGER;
BEGIN
  SELECT COUNT(*) INTO l_total FROM order_statuses;
  SELECT COUNT(*) INTO l_shipped FROM order_statuses WHERE status_cd = 'SHIPPED';
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Total orders: ' || l_total || ', shipped: ' || l_shipped);
END;
/
The two implicit-cursor SELECTs both count rows, but the second applies a predicate before counting, so it returns only the shipped subset. This illustrates the fundamental split between scanning all rows (COUNT(*)) and counting a filtered subset (COUNT(*) ... WHERE). The result for the sample data is 'Total orders: 4, shipped: 2'. Both are scalar INTO queries, so each must return exactly one value or raise TOO_MANY_ROWS/NO_DATA_FOUND; COUNT(*) always returns a single row (zero when no rows match), which is why it is safe here.

Write a PL/SQL procedure that logs each step of a processing loop into a table using a FOR loop with a counter.

CREATE TABLE step_log (step_no NUMBER PRIMARY KEY, step_name VARCHAR2(60), logged_at DATE);
CREATE SEQUENCE seq_step_log START WITH 1 INCREMENT BY 1;

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE log_steps(p_count PLS_INTEGER) IS
BEGIN
  FOR i IN 1 .. p_count LOOP
    INSERT INTO step_log (step_no, step_name, logged_at)
    VALUES (seq_step_log.NEXTVAL, 'STEP_' || i, SYSDATE);
  END LOOP;
  COMMIT;
END log_steps;
/

BEGIN log_steps(5); COMMIT; END;
/
SELECT COUNT(*) AS logged_steps FROM step_log;
The numeric FOR loop iterates from 1 to p_count, and each iteration inserts a row carrying a sequence-generated key and a name built from the counter. The sequence guarantees uniqueness even if the procedure is called repeatedly. A single COMMIT after the loop makes all five inserts atomic. Because step_name derives from the loop index, calling log_steps(5) inserts 'STEP_1' through 'STEP_5'. For very large counts the per-row INSERT is fine here, but a bulk insert built from a CONNECT BY generator would be faster if the loop ever becomes the hot path.

Write a PL/SQL procedure that refreshes a materialized-view-like summary table from a base table using a MERGE, keeping it idempotent.

CREATE TABLE base_sales (sale_id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY, region_cd VARCHAR2(10) NOT NULL, amount NUMBER(12,2) NOT NULL);
INSERT ALL
  INTO base_sales (sale_id, region_cd, amount) VALUES (1, 'EAST', 100.00)
  INTO base_sales (sale_id, region_cd, amount) VALUES (2, 'WEST', 150.00)
  INTO base_sales (sale_id, region_cd, amount) VALUES (3, 'EAST', 200.00)
SELECT * FROM dual;
CREATE TABLE region_summary (region_cd VARCHAR2(10) PRIMARY KEY, total_sales NUMBER(12,2));

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE refresh_region_summary IS
BEGIN
  MERGE INTO region_summary t
  USING (SELECT region_cd, SUM(amount) AS total_sales
         FROM base_sales GROUP BY region_cd) s
  ON (t.region_cd = s.region_cd)
  WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.total_sales = s.total_sales
  WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (region_cd, total_sales)
       VALUES (s.region_cd, s.total_sales);
  COMMIT;
END refresh_region_summary;
/

BEGIN refresh_region_summary; END;
/
SELECT * FROM region_summary ORDER BY region_cd;
MERGE reconciles the summary against the freshly-aggregated source: existing regions update their total, new regions insert. Running the procedure repeatedly produces the same final state, so it is idempotent — a property that makes it safe to invoke from scheduled jobs or after re-runs. The source subquery aggregates base_sales on the fly, so the summary always reflects current data rather than incremental deltas. After the call, region_summary holds EAST=300 and WEST=150 for the sample data.

